 {:seon.task/name "similar-finds-helpers-by-embedding"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (docs/vector.md): semantic search native to the store. EmbeddingGemma-2 runs in process in root through llama.cpp v0.7.0 (native/embed.c, `bin/build embed`; the Q8_0 GGUF fetched once by its sha256 into the main tree's target/cache), as the `:embed` effect kind: a batch kind whose worker opens the model on its first request and keeps it (`seon.fx/worker-proc`'s `:open`, `seon.fx/dispatch`'s `:batch`: what is queued, never a timer). `seon.vector/vectors-proc` embeds each form's, wiki section's and task plan's text that `seon.vector/due` lists, at main's commit notice, round by round, and writes the vectors onto those entities (f16 whole, an int8 128-dim coarse row: `seon.vector/pack`); a form is content-addressed, so it is embedded once. `seon.vector/near` is a Datalog function, [(seon.vector/near $ ?q 10 {:of :form/id}) [[?e ?score]]], over a two-level C search (native/vector.c: int8 coarse scan, f16 rerank of the shortlist). `seon.context/found` fuses the call graph with the forms nearest the task's plan by reciprocal rank; `my.agent/similar` asks it at the REPL. Replaces: v1's MLX HTTP server and its client (`seon.embed`), em1's prototype kernel; the call-graph-only `:found`. Measured: recall@10 of the default levels against exact search 0.998 on Seon's whole text (`bin/check vectors`); helpers recall@10 on 283 commit pairs: call graph 0.042, embeddings 0.495, fused 0.451; one text 5-15 ms, 37k tokens/s batched, whole text embedded in 17-19 s at boot; `near` 4.6 ms in Jolt, 0.08 ms in C. Open: a task-shaped helpers corpus (proofs as sources) to weigh the fusion; the datom copy in `near` past ~100k vectors."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/similar-finds-a-helper-the-call-graph-misses
                      seon.vector-test/the-pyramid-ranks-as-an-exact-search
                      seon.vector-test/near-is-a-relation-in-a-query
                      seon.vector-test/a-text-is-embedded-once
                      seon.vector-test/the-proc-asks-for-what-is-due-and-writes-it
                      seon.fx-test/a-batch-kind-takes-what-is-queued
                      seon.fx-test/a-worker-performs-a-batch-in-one-call-and-opens-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-shows-how-main-diverged"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `:will-merge` (`seon.program/divergence` against the agent's merge base) shows what the branch carries; nothing shows what main changed since that base, so a coming conflict is met only at merge. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §3.6, prototyped): one producer of main's changes since the base intersected with the branch's, shown every turn as {:main-ahead n :conflict [..] :you-call [..] :other n} (about 30 tokens): changed by both, changed on main and called by the branch's changes (:form/refs), the rest counted. Its cost follows the changes since the base, not the program (0.4-0.6 ms with a changed-keys index; a full scan 31 ms per db), so it needs the changed-since primitive of seon.merge/program-cost-follows-what-changed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.merge/program-cost-follows-what-changed"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-turn-names-mains-coming-conflict}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-unrun-proof-is-unknown-in-the-context"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.context` labels a proof with no result :no-assertions unless the run failed as a whole (the :proofs entry, `(get results p (cond ... :else :no-assertions))`), and docs/agent.md#what-you-read tells the agent that means it ran and asserted nothing. A result is absent whenever no report named the test (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`): one not selected or not loaded as well as one with no assertion. Root's judge reads the same absence as :proof-unknown (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`), so agent and judge disagree on one fact. Reproduction (audit): 263 of 265 contexts showed every proof :no-assertions while none had run; agents then loosened a contract. Correct: the context says :unknown, the judge's word. Deletes: the :no-assertions default."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-proof-with-no-result-reads-unknown}}
 {:seon.task/name "old-evaluations-collapse-in-chunks"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: past `:seon.config.render/history-budget`, the history cut in `seon.context/request` drops the oldest record on every turn, so the message after the run sections changes each request and the scrollback misses the provider's prefix cache from then on. Field: SWE-agent's collapse of all but the last 5 observations beat full history by 3 points; Anthropic reports clearing old tool results as the lightest compaction; Manus keeps the context append-only. Correct: at the boundary, collapse the evaluations of all turns but the last N (a config key) to one line each (the form, its status, `(nth (my.turn/evals) k)`), once, so the collapsed prefix stays fixed for the following turns. Measure: cache-hit share per turn from turn 8 on, and turns to green on long runs."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-collapsed-prefix-is-stable-across-turns}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-message-stays-in-the-scrollback"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.context/messages` rebuilds the turn sections every turn as the last user message and drops the earlier ones, so request k+1 is request k minus its last message. DeepSeek makes cache units at the end of each request's input and output, so the newest scrollback turn sits on no unit and hits only through the lazy shared-prefix units, about one turn behind (~5-8k missed tokens a turn, estimated). Correct: each turn's state message stays in the history after that turn's tool messages (made again from its basis, `seon.context/turn-text`); the newest is the current state; older ones may show as a diff against the one before. Prove the prefix stays byte-identical when rendered in two processes (the printer's map order). Measure: hit share per turn on the scripted dry run; v1 got >85%."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/request-k-plus-1-starts-with-request-k-whole}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-red-proofs-calls-are-its-recorded-refs"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: two producers of a function's callees. `my.agent/callees` reads :form/refs (the compiler's facts); `seon.context/callees` (context.clj:101-115), which fills a red proof's :calls (:250), re-reads the proof's source with seon.reader and ns-resolves every symbol in it, so a quoted list or a local named like a var counts as a call. Correct: seon.context/callees is the :form/refs query, kept to program vars that are not tests. Deletes the read-and-resolve."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-red-proofs-calls-are-its-recorded-refs}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-map-reads-the-program-not-the-process"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the review of jolt/dry-run (2026-10-23): ```seon.context/ns-map``` takes each var's arglists from the running process (`find-ns`, `ns-interns`) while its summary comes from the recorded form (```seon.context/summary-of```), so one db rendered in two processes (root, an agent whose branch redefined a var) gives two texts, and the context is meant to be a render of the db value. ```seon.context/summary-of``` too reads the runtime doc first and the recorded form only when there is none. Correct: read both the arglists and the summary from the recorded form, so `ns-map` is a function of db alone. The arglists are every arity's, as `(:arglists (meta v))` gives them: a single-arity defn's params vector, or each arity list's first element (step E run #10's change read only the first vector after the name, so a multi-arity defn showed none). Proof: the map of a db holding a var whose form differs from the process's (its arglists and its docstring) shows the form's arglists and summary, and a two-arity defn shows both arglists."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-map-reads-the-program-not-the-process}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-state-names-what-was-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by step E's runs #8 and #9 (2026-10-23, DeepSeek flash, thinking off; runs seon.context/e1b-map-from-program#1 and e2b-map-from-program#1 on worktree dry-run's root, mains e1 and e2): a design read the same sources turn after turn (`clojure.repl/source` of ns-map in turns 0, 6, 11, 12 of run #8; turns 4-6 of run #9) though every result stood in its scrollback, until the no-progress end. Correct: the current state (```seon.context/current-state```) records what this run has already read (each name and the turn that read it, from the recorded evals), so a re-read is visible as one before the run ends. Recording, never a gate: nothing refuses a read. Proof: the state of a run whose turns read seon.context/ns-map twice names it with both turns."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-state-names-what-was-read}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-ranking-takes-each-key-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured at the REPL on Seon's own program (2,749 forms, 304 sections, 534 tasks; 2026-10-23): a first turn's context (seon.context/render, origin :outside) for task seon.source/an-import-reads-only-changed-files cost 29.8M ticks and 124 MB for an 11 KB text; ns-map (4 namespaces) was 14.9M of it and found 7.6M, though only 5-11 lines of each namespace are shown. Cause: both sorted with clojure.core/sort-by over a key that counts an index (```seon.context/ns-map```'s uses, ```seon.context/found```'s score), and sort-by takes its key on every comparison, so each count ran ~2 n log n times, not n. Correct: take each key once, then sort [key name x] tuples (the same order: the name breaks every tie)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-cost-test/a-namespace-map-counts-each-vars-uses-once seon.context-cost-test/found-scores-each-hit-once seon.context-test/the-map-shows-the-most-used-first-then-by-name}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-first-turns-context-costs-what-it-shows"
  :seon.task/plan "Left by a-ranking-takes-each-key-once (2026-10-23, REPL on Seon's own program): the first turn's context for seon.source/an-import-reads-only-changed-files costs 14.3M ticks and 59 MB for an 11 KB text; of the rest, found's pairs query is ~1.9M, ```seon.ref/references``` ~2.1M, then the cited sections and the printing. Measure each part with a cost proof on a production-shaped db, and bring the render to the work its shown text needs."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "evaluations"
  :seon.task/plan "The model's context is a sequence of real evaluations, rendered from their records by the one printer's :ai face; no hand-built sections (owner, 2026-10-23: \"stick with a system message that we put in via (help) and then a pull and walk render in simple clojure code so there's no magic and we output data for the responses for the agent and it should be cache stable and grow with transcript size mostly except the dynamic portions which should be at the end\"; \"everything is structured as separate evals where we show the forms the agent input (or we generate the help and agents entity walk) and they can clearly see how results come back with timing and individual renders being called so they can see what functions are rendering what data and where the data is so they can update it to update their context generation intuitively\"; \"everything is real and is structured just like the model ran them and recorded and rendered with render functions not manual wiring\"; \"These need to be cachable so we aren't literatlly re-running things\"; \"now can be a named function\"). Root's ruling (decisions.md, 2026-10-23): no live agent runs until the context has a format agents respond well to; each version is scored by agent-loop's judge (about 10 real task contexts, the first move scored blind) and read by Haiku readers (notes/context-reader.md, from one request: {:op :context :task}); keep each change small enough to score often. What exists: seon.context/render builds the system message and the current-state block from hand-built sections (seon.context/current-state, sections, request-text), seon.turn/basis! records a turn's basis, and the context is remade from it. Build, in order, each step scored: (1) the system message is (help)'s text (my.agent/help), stable: markdown, a doc like any wiki page, whose citations seon.ref/parse reads and the context pulls as it pulls any page's (owner, 2026-10-23: \"If help is best a markdown style response and then we parse it for refs that works better I think\"); it says plainly what the views are made of: the walk, the one printer, each value's declared render, and the page's Tailwind (classes in hiccup, regenerated by bin/build), and it cites the docs an agent needs (docs/render.md, docs/references.md for the citation format, the Tailwind setup) at the depth each should be pulled, so they reach every context through the citation mechanism (owner, 2026-10-23: \"Make sure the help text is clear that we are using tailwind and put any refs in there that need ot be pulled in to documentation\"); (2) turn 0 is evaluations the turn loop makes and records as the model's are (origin :context): (help)'s sections it needs, then (my.agent/walk) of the agent's own entity (seon.render/walk: agent, run, task, plan, the plan's citations at their depth, the proofs and how each proof's db is made, from its namespace's fixtures: agent-loop's step E run 11, the model never found them); (3) each later turn appends the model's calls: each evaluation's ;; thought, form, result by the result's own :ai render, and its cost as recorded when it ran (ticks, ms and bytes: seon.turn/a-turn-shows-its-cost-against-its-budget), rendered from that fact, which never changes after its turn, so it is byte-identical every turn and the cache holds (owner's ruling, decisions.md 2026-10-23: cost numbers belong in the context, so models learn how expensive things are and understand warnings and limits); what moves is only in the last evaluation, (now), after the cached prefix, and (now) holds only what changes the agent's next move (owner, 2026-10-23: \"only put really useful shit in (now) and no adding up each eval time isn't useful\"): no sums of the evaluations' costs, no stored totals; a reused result reads exactly like a fresh one (decisions.md 2026-10-23); a recorded evaluation is rendered from its record and never run again, so every earlier turn renders the same bytes and the provider's cache holds (a cost proof: the prefix is byte-identical across turns, and the context grows with the transcript; proofs: an evaluation's cost text is byte-identical at turn n and turn n+1, and what moves (proofs now, turns left) appears only in (now), after the prefix); (3b) byte-stable, verified by the provider (owner, 2026-10-23: \"I want byte stable context gen that we verify is being cached properly by the models\"): a proof renders a run's context at turn n and n+1 and asserts turn n's text is a byte-identical prefix of turn n+1's but for the last evaluation (seon.context-test/the-prefix-stays-byte-identical-across-turns, kept for the new format); each model turn records the tokens of the prefix it shares with the turn before beside the provider's cache-hit tokens (:seon.turn/usage), and a turn whose hit falls short of that prefix by more than the provider's cache block (DeepSeek: 64 tokens) is an open problem fact naming the turn and the gap, never silence; the run's page shows each turn's hit share; one short paid run of a few turns on one task verifies it against the real provider once the format scores well (task a-stable-prefix-is-sent-once); (4) the last evaluation, each turn, is (my.agent/now), the owner's six (2026-10-23, all approved), each present only when it has something to say: am I done (the proofs red now, each failing assertion's expected and actual, what changed since the last turn); what did I break (problems its own change caused: a proof outside its task now red, a contract gap, a stale wiki section; the merge refuses on these); did the world move (main moved, so merge main; another agent changed a var it changed; a task it needs merged); did someone tell me something (new messages); how much room is left (turns left, a warning only near a limit, no tally); what have I already read (so nothing is read twice). Its value is the full data; its :ai render is compact and beautiful, one line a fact, and the agent digs into the value (its handle) for what the render left out (owner: \"I want this compact and beuriful and the agent can dig into the result if they want more detail that maybe the render hid\"). Its pieces: proofs now, what this run has read already (task the-state-names-what-was-read), the one part that breaks the cache; (5) the citations a string holds are shown as citations with what they resolved to, so an agent learns that writing seon.ref's format in any string field brings the cited data in; (6) which render made which part shows as the measured readers find useful, not more (owner: \"mostly helpful data not just a shitload of data\"). Deletes seon.context's section builders (current-state, sections, the request assembly) as each step replaces them; agent-loop's found and ns-map go when the walk's citations replace them (agent-loop: nothing of theirs depends on them)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/walk"}
