 {:seon.task/name "the-request-is-a-conversation-of-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "Step 1 of notes/design-agent-view-2026-10-24.md (approved, docs/decisions.md 2026-10-24). The request is a render of the agent's record (owner, 2026-10-24): each record's :seon.render/ai renders its part as provider-neutral messages (`seon.context/eval-messages`, `seon.context/message-messages`), `seon.context/request` composes them in tx order, and one provider format function, `seon.model/wire-messages`, makes the wire shape; :seon.config.ai/replay-reasoning says whether reasoning goes back. A function-calling conversation: per model turn its prose (the reply's content, stored on its first call's eval), its reasoning replayed exactly as returned, its eval calls, and each call's response as a tool message read from its eval record; the messages a turn took sit before its reply; a form the user evaluated in the agent is a user message carrying its response; the screen ends it. One response per call (`seon.repl/response`, :seon.eval/response): {:handle :value :out :err :ex :ms :ticks :bytes}, each key only when present, the forms run in order and stopped by the first exception, printed by the one printer under its handle result/e<n> (`seon.repl/printed`), which holds the response; n counts the branch's evals, so a handle is unique across restarts. One eval record per call (:seon.eval/*, was :seon.cluster.eval/*; :seon.eval/by, was :origin). tool_choice auto: a prose-only reply ends the turn and the run waits for the user. No ;; reaches the model (the eval tool, docs/agent.md). Deletes: `seon.repl/wrap-print`'s render path, the per-form items and handles, `seon.context/fold-evals`, `shown-eval`, `shown-evals`, `transcript`, the run/turn message split's 'Nothing ran' message, `seon.render.ai/items`, the eval tool's thinking argument, :lead on evals."
